# Task 11 — Kanban Board

**Category:** Data Display
**Difficulty:** near-senior
**Style school:** Neo-Retro

## The problem

A task board with horizontally scrollable columns that snap cleanly into
place as the user scrolls, similar to Trello or Jira.

## Constraints

- Must use CSS scroll snap — no third-party drag/board library for the
  visual layout.

## Key implementation decisions

- The outer row is `overflow-x-auto` with `[scroll-snap-type:x_mandatory]`,
  making horizontal scroll stop only at defined snap points instead of
  anywhere.
- Each column gets `[scroll-snap-align:start]`, so scrolling always settles
  with a column's left edge aligned to the container.
- Columns use `w-72 shrink-0` — a fixed width plus disabling flex-shrink, so
  columns keep their natural width instead of the flex container trying to
  squeeze them all into view (which would defeat the purpose of scrolling).
- Column data intentionally has a different number of cards per column (3,
  2, 1, 3) to confirm columns keep equal width regardless of content amount.

